# Every text file is LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# There was no .gitattributes, so line endings were whatever each
# checkout's core.autocrlf happened to say. That is not a style
# preference here, because this project's tests read its own files:
#
#   - internal/buildpolicy parses go.mod with `^toolchain go1\.\d+$`,
#     which does not match `toolchain go1.26.6\r`. CI reported that
#     go.mod had no toolchain directive, about a go.mod that has one.
#   - internal/docsaudit regenerates docs/configuration.md and
#     docs/modules.md and compares bytes. The generator writes LF, so a
#     CRLF checkout says the committed file is out of date with the code
#     when nothing about the code changed.
#   - internal/specaudit matches multi-line strings against DIVERGENCE.md
#     and splits its prose into sentences. CRLF moved the sentence
#     boundaries and produced two accusations that were not true.
#
# All four passed on every machine this project is developed on and
# failed the first time CI checked out on a Windows runner, where
# core.autocrlf defaults to true. They are not Windows defects; they are
# a checkout setting nothing pinned.
#
# The shell scripts are the other half, and the sharper one: run.sh with
# CRLF is `#!/bin/sh\r`, which no kernel will exec. A Windows clone with
# stock settings produced a `make racecheck` that could not start.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so that "text" is never guessed at for them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gz binary
*.xz binary
*.zip binary
